# Review loop validation (2026-10-14)

Public behavior: `createCommandRouter(...).reviewFeature(options)` runs both review
roles on one frozen SHA, persists round/repair/dispute history, and reports a terminal
outcome in status, an adapter toast and the dashboard. No target merge, push or PR
creation is performed by the coordinator.

TDD evidence: the first clean-round test failed because the coordinator module did
not exist, then passed after its implementation. The persistent-defect test failed
with only one round, then passed with exactly three rounds and two fresh repair
worktrees. An intermediate skipped-check regression failed as `verified`, then
passed as `incomplete-validation` after retaining checks on each repair attempt.
The infrastructure-budget regression failed with two functional invocations after
three infrastructure failures, then passed with one invocation. Formatting, package
exports and wiki updates are non-behavior changes and have no separate red test.

Demonstration: the two planted code/UI defects test starts a real feature branch,
creates two dedicated repairs, writes regression tests in fresh worktrees, observes
real Bun red and green results, validates each candidate and fast-forwards only the
feature branch via an injected merger fixture. Both reviewers pass round two. Each
repair uses one attempt; expected red failures do not consume attempts. The target
ref and remotes remain unchanged. The persistent-defect test performs two repairs
and stops with unresolved findings at round three; no fourth review or third repair
cycle is dispatched.

The dedicated integration suite also covers repair exhaustion, deduplication,
withdrawn/retained/evidence-less disputes, unavailable functional review, passing
reviewers with skipped or incomplete validation, stale output, terminal lock release,
independent SDK adapter sessions and an exhausted feature-wide infrastructure budget.
Chromium checks compare all four outcome labels and branch/SHA against persisted
status and projections without browser mutation controls.

Limitations: executor/merger/scheduler and active-run lock predecessors are absent.
Production callers must supply the `RepairPipeline` and lock-release boundary;
without a repair pipeline blocking findings terminate unresolved. The coordinator
cannot attest a production merger implementation that does not exist. The UI
repair demonstration tests a rendered-label module, not live browser repair by an
agent. Dashboard checks use real Chromium with deterministic reviewer fixtures;
live-provider review, publication, Linux and Windows have not been tested here.
No package was published or external message sent.

Command results on pinned Bun 1.4.3:
- `bun run typecheck`: passed.
- `bun run lint`: passed (40 source/test/script files).
- `bun test tests/integration`: 53 passed, one explicitly gated real
  agent-browser check skipped, zero failures (54 total).
- `bun run test:browser -- tests/browser/review-report.test.ts tests/browser/dashboard.test.ts`:
  five Chromium tests passed, including four review-report outcomes.
- `bun test tests/integration/13-review-loop.test.ts`: all 12 tests passed
  (98 assertions).
- `bun run build`: passed, including declaration output and dashboard assets.
# Bounded review and repair

## Summary
The public `reviewFeature` command boundary joins code and functional review on one pinned commit. It persists review history and a final report for OpenCode status and the read-only dashboard.

## Key behavior
- Both reviewers settle before repairs start. Rejected reviewers retain any already persisted findings.
- Three total review rounds, three attempts per repair, and three feature-wide functional infrastructure failures are separate budgets. Expected TDD red runs stay inside an executor attempt.
- Equivalent criterion/repair pairs become one repair task with both sources, requirements context and evidence. The approved issue graph is unchanged.
- Each attempt gets a fresh detached worktree and durable intent. The supplied executor/merger pipeline must perform TDD and approved candidate validation. Integration results must identify a clean candidate, final SHA and all required checks.
- One clarification exchange per originating finding per round; withdrawal requires reviewer evidence and justification. Missing evidence retains the blocker. Disputes grant no extra round.
- Confirmed blockers take precedence over unavailable review. Missing review is unverified; missing or skipped validation is incomplete-validation. Intermediate skipped checks remain disclosed after later passing checks.
- Terminal reports persist before the lifecycle owner releases its lock. Concurrent owners and stale reviewer completions cannot finalize a workflow. Interrupted operations require explicit recovery.
- Branches, review/repair worktrees and logs remain available. The coordinator does not push, open PRs or merge a target branch.

## Code references
- `src/review/coordinator.ts` — round orchestration and bounded attempt intents.
- `src/review/repairs.ts` — repair pipeline interface, deduplication and disputes.
- `src/reporting/completion.ts` — evidence-based final outcomes.
- `src/state/run-store.ts` — persisted workflow and owner/revision guards.
- `src/dashboard/projections.ts`, `src/dashboard/web/App.tsx` — sanitized review/report visibility.
- `tests/integration/13-review-loop.test.ts`, `tests/browser/review-report.test.ts` — termination paths and report consistency.

## Related
- [Independent code review](independent-code-review.md)
- [Functional review](functional-review.md)
- [Read-only dashboard](read-only-dashboard.md)

## Notes
Executor, merger, scheduler and active-run lock implementations are absent in this checkout. `RepairPipeline` and `releaseActiveRun` are explicit integration boundaries; missing repair support terminates unresolved, never as a successful fix. Production repair execution, reviewer clarification sessions and live-provider/cross-platform checks remain unverified. The real Git/TDD demonstration uses an injected test pipeline.

